<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odaenathus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 220–267</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odaenathus was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>. After the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> in 260, he attacked the retreating Persian army, suppressed the usurpers Macrianus and Quietus, and raided Sasanian territory as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> recognised him as corrector of the whole East. He was assassinated around 267, and Zenobia then ruled in the name of their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odaenathus.jpg" alt="Odaenathus" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald Ingholt, Henri Seyrig and Jean Starcky, followed by linguistic remarks by André Caquot. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tessera_from_Palmyra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
